00:55:31,890 --> 00:55:35,110
It's not making your head hurt to think about it.

597
00:55:35,510 --> 00:55:41,110
But I think with this, what's clearly the case when she presents that Declaration of the Rights of Women.

598
00:55:42,150 --> 00:55:55,270
You know, when she unveils it, the ridicule, the contempt, the disbelief that greets it is a sign that a lot of people just simply cannot imagine a world in which women exercise political power.

599
00:55:55,870 --> 00:55:56,830
Including women.

600
00:55:57,030 --> 00:55:58,330
Yeah, including women themselves.

601
00:55:58,430 --> 00:55:58,930
Exactly.

602
00:55:59,030 --> 00:56:07,250
I don't want to say prisoners of the same imagination, because that casts people in the past as somehow lesser than us.

603
00:56:07,370 --> 00:56:08,510
We're more enlightened.

604
00:56:08,850 --> 00:56:11,610
And I don't generally like that kind of language.

605
00:56:11,670 --> 00:56:12,130
But I think it's a good thing.

606
00:56:12,130 --> 00:56:19,490
And I think it is fair to say that just as we are trapped by our own preconceptions in ways that we don't even recognize, they are absolutely trapped by theirs.

607
00:56:19,830 --> 00:56:40,430
But I think it also suggests that we are beneficiaries of, you know, the events that we're describing in this series in ways that we may not appreciate, that we may mistake for truths so self-evident that they don't need to be argued for, that that's not what they are at all.

608
00:56:40,510 --> 00:56:42,110
That they are, in fact, kind of.

609
00:56:42,910 --> 00:56:58,930
Radical, intellectual, ideological innovations that ultimately succeed because we come to feel, yeah, they're absolutely right, but when they are first proposed, just seem absolutely kind of mad.

610
00:56:59,090 --> 00:57:06,730
Well, Tom, there are lots of people in the world right now, when we're recording this, who would listen to this episode and would say they are mad.

611
00:57:06,930 --> 00:57:11,950
I mean, there are people right now, you know, in the people who are currently administering Afghanistan.

612
00:57:12,130 --> 00:57:18,590
Or indeed Iran, who would say, you know, a lot of what Olympe de Gouges was arguing was bonkers.

613
00:57:18,890 --> 00:57:21,810
So maybe we shouldn't be entirely complacent about it, I guess.

614
00:57:22,090 --> 00:57:23,370
Yes, well, it's, yes.

615
